      "body": "## Overview\n\nvLLM CI on ROCm should succeed with `VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER=1`, since most model serving commands are prefixed with `VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER=1`. This PR disables Aiter dynamic quantization in vLLM when using fp8 fnuz.\n\n## Claims\n\nAfter this PR, the test `tests/kernels/test_concat_mla_q.py::test_concat_mla_q_fp8_nope_and_rope` passes with `VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER=1` set.\n\n## Validation\n\nRun the test\n\n```bash\nVLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER=1 python3 -m pytest \\\n  -v -s tests/kernels/test_concat_mla_q.py \\\n  -k test_concat_mla_q_fp8_nope_and_rope\n```\n\nThe following parameter combinations fail before this PR and pass after:\n\n| Parameters | Before | After |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `True-0-False` | FAIL | PASS |\n| `True-0-True` | FAIL | PASS |\n| `True-64-False` | FAIL | PASS |\n\nThe first `True` enables testing the custom op `mla_decode_concat_quant_fp8`. https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/blob/0ca531ba64c61af32accb3b52226e8d819c6a49b/tests/kernels/test_concat_mla_q.py#L176\n\n## Details\n\nAiter's per-tensor FP8 quantization kernels saturate float8_e4m3fnuz outputs at +/-240, while vLLM's own convention clamps to +/-224.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [ ] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [X] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [X] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [X] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [X] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [X] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n",
      "changedFiles": 1,
